.\" dropbearkey(1) for Amix: Dropbear 2026.94 as this package builds it.
.\" From upstream's page by Gerrit Pape and Matt Johnston (MIT, see
.\" /opt/amix/doc/dropbear/LICENSE), rewritten in -man for the system's nroff.
.TH DROPBEARKEY 1 "1 November 2026" "dropbear 2026.94"
.SH NAME
dropbearkey \- make a key for dropbear and dbclient
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dropbearkey
.B \-t
.B ed25519
|
.B rsa
.B \-f
.I file
[
.B \-s
.I bits
]
.br
.B dropbearkey
.B \-y
.B \-f
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dropbearkey
makes a private key, from
.BR /dev/urandom ,
writes it to
.I file
(which must not exist yet), readable by its owner only, and prints the
public key and its fingerprint.
The key is a host key for
.BR dropbear (8)
or a user's key for
.BR dbclient (1).
Keys are not encrypted, so the file's mode is all that protects one.
.TP
.BI \-t " type"
.B ed25519
(the one to use: small and fast) or
.BR rsa ,
for a peer that knows no ed25519.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
The private key's file.
.TP
.BI \-s " bits"
The size of an RSA key, a multiple of 8 (default 3072).
Making one takes minutes on a 68030.
.TP
.B \-y
Print the public key and fingerprint of the key in
.IR file ;
the public line is what goes into a server's
.BR ~/.ssh/authorized_keys .
.SH EXAMPLES
A key to log in with, and its public half for the server:
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
$ mkdir -m 700 ~/.ssh
$ dropbearkey -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/id_dropbear
$ dropbearkey -y -f ~/.ssh/id_dropbear | grep '^ssh-'
.ft R
.fi
.PP
An RSA host key, made by hand instead of by the boot script:
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
# dropbearkey -t rsa -f /etc/dropbear/host_rsa
.ft R
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dbclient (1),
.BR dropbear (8)
